sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch11-16> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "At the present day many European species have become naturalised and partly displaced the natives in La Plata and to a lesser degree in Australia, whereas extremely few southern-hemisphere species have become naturalised anywhere in Europe.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Northern species naturalise far more often in the south than the reverse";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter XI, “Geographical Distribution”; section “Dispersal during the Glacial period co-extensive with the world”. Scope: naturalised plants and animals exchanged between Europe and southern-hemisphere regions"
}
